	// The values are from n-m+1 to n.
	// We need to find the (k-1)-th permutation (0-indexed) of these m values.
	// Let's generate the permutation of indices 0..m-1 corresponding to the (k-1)-th permutation.
	// The actual values are: value at position (n-m+1 + idx) = (n-m+1) + perm[idx]
	// where perm is a permutation of 0..m-1.

